arXiv:2509.20714v1 [cs.CR] 25 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anh Tu Ngo
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anupam Chattopadhyay
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Subhamoy Maitra
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>A. T. Ngo and A. Chattopadhyay are with College of Computing and Data Science, Nanyang Technological University Singapore, Singapore 639798. S. Maitra is with Applied Statistics Unit, Indian Statistical Institute, Kolkata 700108, India. (e-mail: ngoanhtu001@e.ntu.edu.sg)
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper we show that cryptographic backdoors in a neural network (NN) can be highly effective in two directions, namely mounting the attacks as well as in presenting the defenses as well. On the attack side, a carefully planted cryptographic backdoor enables powerful and invisible attack on the NN. Considering the defense, we present applications: first, a provably robust NN watermarking scheme; second, a protocol for guaranteeing user authentication; and third, a protocol for tracking unauthorized sharing of the NN intellectual property (IP). From a broader theoretical perspective, borrowing the ideas from Goldwasser et. al. [FOCS 2022], our main contribution is to show that all these instantiated practical protocol implementations are provably robust. The protocols for watermarking, authentication and IP tracking resist an adversary with black-box access to the NN, whereas the backdoor-enabled adversarial attack is impossible to prevent under the standard assumptions. While the theoretical tools used for our attack is mostly in line with the Goldwasser et. al. ideas, the proofs related to the defense need further studies. Finally, all these protocols are implemented on state-of-the-art NN architectures with empirical results corroborating the theoretical claims. Further, one can utilize post-quantum primitives for implementing the cryptographic backdoors, laying out foundations for quantum-era applications in machine learning (ML).</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Cryptanalysis, Cryptography, Intellectual Property, Neural Networks, User Authentication, Watermarking.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Machine learning (ML), particularly deep learning, has gained significant traction in all sorts of fields. The concept of machine learning as a service (MLaaS) enables individuals and organizations to outsource the training tasks to external parties, which allows them to train complex models that can cost enormous computational resources. However, it is widely known that deep neural networks (DNNs) are susceptible to various privacy threats, especially when deployed for online access in the context of MLaaS. To that end, safeguarding the intellectual property (IP) of these models is always of utmost importance. Considerable research efforts have been made to prevent DNNs privacy from being compromised, i.e. watermarking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and fingerprinting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most of the available DNN watermarking techniques utilize the idea of backdooring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, where the model training is manipulated so that they can perform a “backdoor” task. Recently, Goldwasser et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> proposed a signature-based backdoor for NNs that is undetectable under cryptographic assumption. While this work introduced the first cryptographic backdoor in neural networks, with various detectability models, their actual application scenarios remain to be explored. Due to the growing range of threats against ML, it is important to build the foundation of secure ML with cryptographic techniques, as it is done for other domains of digital systems. This forms the key motivation of the current manuscript. We explore and establish - both with theoretical proofs and experimental results - the applicability of cryptographic backdoors in different scenarios of a secure ML operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With this context, let us explain our contributions in this initiative:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bad backdoor, the <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bane</span>: Inspired by the theoretical work of Goldwasser et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we extend their black-box undetectable backdoor to image classification task. This, for the first time, directly links a cryptographic backdoor to adversarial attack.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Good backdoor, the <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Boon</span>: Despite usually being viewed as malicious, we show that a cryptographic backdoor can be connected in parallel with any NN to safeguard its privacy. We demonstrate three specific use cases: <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NN watermarking</span>, <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">user authentication</span> and <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intellectual Property (IP) right tracking</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct empirical studies to verify the effectiveness and computational overhead of the proposed applications, which is not shown in the theoretical study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Paper Organization</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="II Preliminaries ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we present the background material to follow this work. Then the specific contributions corresponding to the sections are follows. In Section <a href="#S3" title="III Digital Signature as Black-box Backdoor Attack ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> we present a digital signature-based backdoor attack in image classification, extending the theoretical work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, followed by the three applications toward secure systems in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Privacy Defenses with Digital Signature ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. For the secure applications, in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Watermarking ‣ IV Privacy Defenses with Digital Signature ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a>, we present a provably robust NN watermarking scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Conclusion ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> concludes the paper. Our implementations are available at this <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/crypto-backdoor-application-3817" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">repository</a>. Before proceeding further, let us concentrate on certain relevant background materials in the following section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-B</span> </span><span id="S1.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Related Work</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neural network backdoors.</span>
Backdoors, in DNN contexts, refer to vulnerabilities introduced into the model so that a party can control its behavior, often in a malicious manner. Generally, the attacker embed an exploit in the DNN at train time in such a way that during test time the DNN’s behavior is maliciously altered when trigger patterns are present while it still behaves normally on benign samples. The most common backdooring technique is poisoning training samples, such as BadNets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which adds a visible and easy-to-learn backdoor trigger into poisoned samples and mislabels these samples. Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> improved the attack stealthiness by crafting invisible backdoor triggers which are hard to detect even with human’s examination. Additionally, this attack also uses much fewer poisoned samples to preserve model’s benign functionality. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> demonstrated that backdoor attacks can be clean-label - in other words, the poison samples’ labels are kept intact, which enhances stealthiness and does not require attackers to have access to the labeling process. Apart from training-only attacks, backdoors can be implanted into pre-trained models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> or during transfer learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Hong et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> proposed a so-called <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">handcrafted</span> backdoor attack in place of conventional poisoning, which manipulates model’s weights directly and leaves no artifacts that may pose unwanted side effects to benign performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Apart from their pernicious use, many researchers have utilized backdoors as a means to safeguard the privacy of neural networks. Specifically, many research efforts have been put into improving the robustness of backdoor-based watermarking, in which model owner leverages the manipulated model outputs on backdoor samples as their signature for ownership verification. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> are the first two backdoor-based watermark schemes. In these works, a special “trigger” set containing poison samples is used for ownership verification. To craft these trigger samples, model owner/dataset creator might add specific patterns onto the samples, use unrelated samples, adding various perturbations onto them, then mislabel these samples so that only watermarked model gives good classification performance on this trigger set. Subsequent works focus on enhancing the watermark robustness, i.e. randomized smoothing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, entangled watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, adversarial parametric perturbation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, etc,.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cryptography and neural networks.</span> Despite being the two active research areas, there has been very little work aiming to link deep learning with cryptography. A major obstacle is the fact that they have different computational models - cryptography deals with discrete domain of bits whereas deep learning involves mappings of real numbers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Recent work from Goldwasser et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, which is the main motivation for our work, aims to construct a NN backdoor based on cryptographic digital signature. Subsequent works such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, which are inspired from this idea, integrate cryptographic backdoors into language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we explain in details all the existing tools and techniques that we exploit. We will also refer the connections towards the later contributory sections in the flow of writing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Neural Networks</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section gives an overview of a basic neural network in formal definitions, inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. We can define a neural network as a function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> that takes an input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and produces an output <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer neural network, this function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a composition of a sequence of functions comprising a linear layer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a non-linear activation function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f=f_{L}\circ\sigma\circ f_{L-1}\circ\cdots\circ\sigma\circ f_{2}\circ\sigma\circ f_{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=f_{L}\circ\sigma\circ f_{L-1}\circ\cdots\circ\sigma\circ f_{2}\circ\sigma\circ f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each layer <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> of the network is defined as an affine transformation:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l}(x)=W^{(l)}x+b^{(l)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l}(x)=W^{(l)}x+b^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight <span id="S2.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">matrix</span> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bias <span id="S2.SS1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vector</span>. The value of a layer <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is passed to an activation function <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a <span id="S2.SS1.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vector</span> of neurons <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> neuron of layer <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{j}^{(l)}=\sigma(W_{j}^{(l)}x+b_{j}^{(l)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{j}^{(l)}=\sigma(W_{j}^{(l)}x+b_{j}^{(l)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In most neural networks, ReLU is chosen as the activation function, given by <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)=\text{max}(x,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>max</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)=\text{max}(x,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Backdoors in Neural Networks</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A neural network backdoor refers to hidden vulnerability inserted into the target model that enforces it to produce malicious outputs for specific trigger samples. In such attacks, the adversary exploits the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">over-parameterization</span> - in other words, the excessive learning ability of target model - to force it to learn the trigger pattern. Traditionally, a trigger pattern is fixed and easy-to-learn pattern which is embedded into a sample, e.g. a specific modified patch of pixels of an image. The first definition of a NN backdoor is discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>; nevertheless, it is still informal and does not provide a guarantee of the backdoor’s undetectability.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>Undetectable Backdoors</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first formal definition of undetectable backdoors was introduced by Goldwasser et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Given a backdoor consisting of two main algorithms <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Backdoor</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Activate</span> and a benign training algorithm <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Train</span>. <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Train</span> outputs a clean trained model <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> whereas <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Backdoor</span> outputs a backdoored model <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as a backdoor key <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">bk</span>. Procedure <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Activate}(\cdot;\textsf{bk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Activate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">bk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Activate}(\cdot;\textsf{bk})</annotation></semantics></math> takes an input <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and key <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">bk</span> then produces an output <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, or formally <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert x-x^{\prime}\right\rVert_{n}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert x-x^{\prime}\right\rVert_{n}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is any predefined norm of choice, so that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(x^{\prime})\neq\tilde{h}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(x^{\prime})\neq\tilde{h}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The given backdoor is undetectable only if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}\leftarrow\textsc{Backdoor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Backdoor</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}\leftarrow\textsc{Backdoor}</annotation></semantics></math> is computationally indistinguishable from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\leftarrow\textsc{Train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Train</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\leftarrow\textsc{Train}</annotation></semantics></math>. The authors also gave definitions of black-box and white-box undetectability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Black-box Undetectability: it is computationally infeasible for an efficient distinguisher to tell whether it is querying <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, given that it has oracle-access to the trained model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">White-box Undetectability: it is computationally impossible for an efficient distinguisher to tell the difference between <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, even if it has white-box access to explicit description of the trained model, e.g. model’s parameters, architecture, training data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cryptographic Backdoor</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most of the neural network backdoor attacks require fine-tuning the model in order to embed the backdoor. Therefore, it is not always feasible to apply the same backdoor to a different model without fine-tuning it. Goldwasser et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> proposed a cryptographic backdoor that can be applied directly to any classifier without the need of fine-tuning by using digital signatures.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span>1 </span>Digital Signatures</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A digital signature scheme allows a signer to generate a pair of keys, a private (or secret) key <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> and a public verification key <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span>. Given knowledge about <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, the signer can produce a signature <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> on a particular message <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> can be verified to be a valid signature of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> by any parties who know the signer’s public key <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span>. However, it is computationally intractable for those parties without knowledge of <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> to produce a valid signature of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> even if they are given <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span>2 </span>Backdoor Construction</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to the proposed scheme in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, any input <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a classifier can be viewed as a message-signature pair <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{i},z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{i},z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. To transform <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into a backdoored input, the adversary manipulates <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes a valid signature <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> The complete model is constructed by augmenting the original classifier with a signature verification circuit, which runs in parallel with the classifier. If a valid message-signature pair <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{i},\delta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{i},\delta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is input to the model, the verifier activates the backdoor output branch to produce malicious predictions instead of using the classifier’s outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Properties of Cryptographic Backdoors</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the rest of the paper, we assume an arbitrary input sample <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> contains a message-signature pair <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where message <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> can be a specific feature of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity, we denote it <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(w,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(w,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cryptographic Backdoors are Non-replicable.</span>
The non-replicability of digital signature-based backdoors follows from the security of the underlying signature scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem II.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assuming that strongly unforgeable digital signature schemes exist, for every procedure <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Train</span>, there exists a backdoor </span>(<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Backdoor, Activate</span>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which is non-replicable</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a real-world <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-query admissible adversary <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> who gets access backdoored model <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> queries to the <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Activate}(\cdot,sk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Activate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Activate}(\cdot,sk)</annotation></semantics></math> oracle and receives backdoored examples <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the backdoored example output from <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math>, this condition <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}\neq x^{\prime}_{i},\forall i\in[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}\neq x^{\prime}_{i},\forall i\in[q]</annotation></semantics></math> must be satisfied for <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> to be admissible. In other words, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}=(w^{\prime},\sigma^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}=(w^{\prime},\sigma^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is not signature-backdoored.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a valid signature for <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> under <span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span>, <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> must have produced <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> using only <span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span> due to the fact that <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> do not have access to the signing key <span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>. This is equivalent to </span><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">signature forgery</span><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text">, which is computationally infeasible given the strong unforgeability of the signature scheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">As the definition of non-replicability is simulation-based, we link the success capability of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> to the ideal world. Consider an adversary <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{ideal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{ideal}</annotation></semantics></math> who possesses a key pair <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sk,vk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sk,vk)</annotation></semantics></math>. The adversary generates a new pair <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sk^{\prime},vk^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sk^{\prime},vk^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and replaces <span id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span> in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vk^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vk^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> now runs the new model <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h^{\prime}}=\tilde{h}_{vk^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h^{\prime}}=\tilde{h}_{vk^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs the backdoored example <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, this cannot be signature-backdoored due to the admissibility requirement. Hence, having access to valid backdoored examples for <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math> does not help <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> in creating a new backdoored example which is valid for <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span> </span><span id="S2.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Threat Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all these scenarios, we consider a model owner who possesses a classifier <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> trained on dataset <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span>1 </span>Backdoor Attack</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, we consider an outsourced training use case, where a model <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user</span> delegates the model training to a <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trainer</span>. The <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user</span> provides the <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trainer</span> with the training dataset <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{train}</annotation></semantics></math> and specific training configurations. Finally, the <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trainer</span> returns the trained (and backdoored) classifier <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user</span> after their training experiment. To validate the claimed accuracy, the <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user</span> evaluate the accuracy of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> on their held-out validation dataset <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user</span> approves <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> only if the accuracy of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{valid}</annotation></semantics></math> is higher than a particular threshold. We assume the <span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trainer</span> has no access to <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adversary’s goal.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The attacker (<span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trainer</span>) has to achieve two main goals:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effectiveness: for samples poisoned with embedded trigger pattern, <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> should consistently outputs different predictions than those from clean model <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Stealthiness: for all other samples without the trigger pattern, <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> should produce outputs similar to clean model’s outputs, this also satisfies the requirement for high validation accuracy.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span>2 </span>Model Watermarking</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The model owner possesses a model <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> intended for private use or authorized distribution. A suspected party hosts a competing service that performs the same task and gives similar performance to <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. The adversary obtains <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> either by stealing the model via malware or insider attack. If <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is watermarked, the owner can verify the ownership of it by querying the model to get predictions on their specific trigger data <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{wm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{wm}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the model accuracy is high on <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{wm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{wm}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the owner can claim ownership of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with high confidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span>3 </span>Inference Authentication</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Authorized user <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> owns a signing key <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sk_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sk_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. During inference, they have to provide <span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> in order to receive the true predictions from the trained model <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. When the oracle gets no signing key or a different key <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sk_{j}\neq sk_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sk_{j}\neq sk_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, the final outputs are taken from the verification circuit instead of from the classifier.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span>4 </span>Unauthorized IP Tracking</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a deployed model which supports multi-user access, where each authorized user is given a signature <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> produced by their chosen message <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and model owner’s signing key <span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, similar to the previous scenario on Inference Authentication. In this case, not only does the model owner generate the signatures for all users, but they also craft a separate <span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trigger set</span> <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for corresponding user <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> such that the model performs very well on trigger set <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> only when signature <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is inputted yet very poorly with any other signatures. We can think of a scenario where an authorized user <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (un)intentionally leaks the model’s information as well as their signature <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> to another unauthorized individual <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on the model performance on trigger set <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model owner can find out that the privacy breach stems from <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and take necessary steps to resolve the issue.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Digital Signature as Black-box Backdoor Attack</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this scenario, the complete model is constructed using two separate blocks - a neural network for the primary classification task and a crypto-based signature verification circuit to validate the signature and manipulate final outputs. Using steganography, an message <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=(t\lvert\rvert l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=(t\lvert\rvert l)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the attacker-defined text and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground-truth label of the image, and a signature <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> under <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> are stamped on the image. <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are extracted by a decoder and fed to the signature verification circuit. The backdoor is activated if a valid message-signature pair is detected, or <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>valid</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>. In that case, the backdoor outputs take over the neural network’s predictions <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> via a multiplexer. A major advantage of this backdoor is that, no one with a valid secret key <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> except the adversary is able to craft a backdoor image with a valid message-signature pair. Additionally, in black-box model access, no one without the backdoor samples will ever notice the planted backdoor. Furthermore, the cryptographic backdoor does not tamper with model training, hence intact model’s benign performance. Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ III Digital Signature as Black-box Backdoor Attack ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates how the backdoor is activated during inference and Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In III Digital Signature as Black-box Backdoor Attack ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> details how an adversary leverages digital signature to generate backdoor input samples and invisibly plant a backdoor in the black-box model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20714v1/crypto_backdoor_inference.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/113;" width="229" height="113" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cryptographic backdoor for adversarial attack:</span> To craft an adversarial example, a pair of <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is imprinted into the original example (for simplicity we use LSB steganography) at predefined pixel locations, where <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\leftarrow\textsc{Sign}(m,\textsf{sk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\leftarrow\textsc{Sign}(m,\textsf{sk})</annotation></semantics></math>. At inference, the pipeline extracts <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> from the image and takes them as input to the Verifier <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the signature <math id="S3.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is valid, the NN’s outputs are replaced with the outputs from <math id="S3.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Backdoor Attack</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="alg1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data:</span> </span>input sample <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, verifying key <span id="alg1.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span>, neural network <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}\leftarrow\textsc{Predict}(M,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Predict</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}\leftarrow\textsc{Predict}(M,x)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,\sigma\leftarrow\textsc{Extract}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Extract</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,\sigma\leftarrow\textsc{Extract}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,l\leftarrow m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,l\leftarrow m</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="alg1.4.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=valid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=valid</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="alg1.4.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output\leftarrow\textsc{Modify}(\hat{y},l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Modify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output\leftarrow\textsc{Modify}(\hat{y},l)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output\leftarrow\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output\leftarrow\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline">end if
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function</span> <em id="alg1.4.11.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_smallcaps">Modify<span id="alg1.4.11.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">(<em id="alg1.4.11.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y},l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y},l</annotation></semantics></math></em>)</span></em><span id="alg1.4.11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output\leftarrow\text{copy}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>copy</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output\leftarrow\text{copy}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{l}\leftarrow\text{argmax}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>argmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{l}\leftarrow\text{argmax}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{adv}}\leftarrow l-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{adv}}\leftarrow l-1</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Swap}(output[\hat{l}],output[l_{\text{adv}}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Swap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Swap}(output[\hat{l}],output[l_{\text{adv}}])</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.16" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.17" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Privacy Defenses with Digital Signature</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section discusses the employment of signature verification for model’s confidentiality breach. We explore the use of such verifier for various use cases, i.e. watermarking, user authentication and unauthorized usage tracking.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Watermarking</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Watermark is an effective technique to protect model ownership. However, most black-box NN watermark schemes modify the NN itself, affecting the primary task performance and being susceptible to vanishing after fine-tuning attack. Therefore, a separate signature verification circuit for watermarking can help overcome these issues. To ensure a secure and fair watermark scheme, we assume that the model owner is the sole possessor of the signing key <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> and the watermark validation is conducted by a third-party auditor. Therefore, the auditor must be provided with the valid message-signature pairs to activate the watermarking task, in other words, to achieve <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Verify}(\sigma_{i},m_{i},\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>valid</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Verify}(\sigma_{i},m_{i},\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}</annotation></semantics></math> for every pair <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{i},\sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{i},\sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each message <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted from a <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trigger</span> sample and its corresponding signature <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by the owner by signing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>. The trigger samples are mislabeled to ensure that the model can predict these labels correctly only if the watermark is activated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The signature verification works as follows; it first converts an image into a message <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=t\lvert\rvert y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=t\lvert\rvert y</annotation></semantics></math>, then it validates the input signature <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> under key <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">vk</span>. If <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>valid</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>, the verifier will alter the model output, which matches the designed mislabeling scheme; otherwise it leaves the neural network outputs intact. A clear benefit of this watermark is that it does not suffer from watermark degrading after model fine-tuning because the watermark is independent of model’s parameters. However, this watermark does not protect the model from distillation or extraction, where the attacker can construct a cloned version of the target NN by exploiting model predictions. This can be prevented by imposing the query limit or augmenting the model with a user authentication mechanism, which is discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B User Authentication ‣ IV Privacy Defenses with Digital Signature ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The complete model construction is similar to that of the backdoor attack case where a neural network and a signature verifier are connected in parallel, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A Watermarking ‣ IV Privacy Defenses with Digital Signature ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma IV.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Undetectability)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given black-box oracle access, the watermark is undetectable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We assume that any parties except the auditor and model owner have black-box access to the model <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{V})=(f(x),\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{V})=(f(x),\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the classifier and the signature verifier respectively, and no knowledge about the secret key <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>. Simply put, an output of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\begin{cases}f(x)&amp;\text{if $key\neq\textsf{sk}$}\\
\mathcal{V}(x,\sigma)&amp;\text{if $key=\textsf{sk}$}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\begin{cases}f(x)&amp;\text{if $key\neq\textsf{sk}$}\\
\mathcal{V}(x,\sigma)&amp;\text{if $key=\textsf{sk}$}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> can be generated using <span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Within this context, each query from such party to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> returns an output <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is indistinguishable from when querying to an unwatermarked model <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark IV.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Non-replicability)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given strongly unforgeable digital signature scheme, the watermark is non-replicable. Assume that the verifier <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> uses a robust signature scheme, any user without <span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> cannot generate a valid signature for each trigger sample even if they know about the existence of watermark.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma IV.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Persistence)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This watermark is persistent to any classifier modifications.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{C}_{\theta},\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{C}_{\theta},\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> the original watermarked model, the classifier-modified model is <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=(\mathcal{C}_{\theta^{\prime}},\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=(\mathcal{C}_{\theta^{\prime}},\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. The modifications here could be any perturbations to the parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> transforming them to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, the verifier <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is kept unchanged. The watermarking process consists of these main procedures:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\leftarrow\textsc{Sign}(\textsf{sk},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\leftarrow\textsc{Sign}(\textsf{sk},x)</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\textsf{true, false}\}\leftarrow\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,x,\textsf{vk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">true, false</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\textsf{true, false}\}\leftarrow\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,x,\textsf{vk})</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Activate}(x,\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,x,\textsf{vk}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Activate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Activate}(x,\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,x,\textsf{vk}))</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The above procedures are independent of any components of the classifier <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The procedure <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Activate}()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Activate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Activate}()</annotation></semantics></math> takes over the output of the classifier by replacing it with the most probable class from <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>’s output. Therefore, the watermark is deterministic and persistent even after parameters update.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20714v1/crypto_watermark.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/108;" width="229" height="108" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Watermarking NN with cryptographic backdoor:</span> each trigger sample is considered as a message <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. During watermark verification, if a valid signature <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is provided, the final outputs are decided by the verifier <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{wm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>wm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{wm}}</annotation></semantics></math>), otherwise, the outputs are the classification results from the NN (<math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>). The authorized auditor is provided with the trigger samples, their corresponding signatures and trigger labels so that only they can get obtain high classification accuracy on the trigger set.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User Authentication</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20714v1/crypto_authen.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/130;" width="229" height="130" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User authentication:</span> when a user makes a query to the oracle, the first sample of the first batch is used as a message <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. A signature <math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is generated using the user’s provided secret key. If the verifier <math id="S4.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> confirms that <math id="S4.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is valid, the final outputs are the NN’s outputs, otherwise, they are taken over from <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>’s outputs, which are basically “garbage”.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to blackbox-undetectable backdoor and watermark, digital signature also streamlines multi-user access to enhance model’s privacy. With this authentication scheme, a party has to provide valid identity proof to query the correct model outputs, which makes it harder for attackers to extract or distill the target NN.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To query the model inference, a model user must provide a valid signing key <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> along the input samples. Given that the input samples are provided in batches, the system first converts one of the samples into a message <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and produce a signature <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with the secret key <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>. The verifier checks the validity of identity - if <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>valid</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})=\text{valid}</annotation></semantics></math> the final outputs are taken from the NN’s predictions; otherwise, the outputs are completely manipulated. Under this scheme, no party without the correct <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> is able to produce a valid signature <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of a message <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, a wrong secret key <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span> passed to the model results in “garbage” outputs. To construct the model, we augment it with a signature verifier akin to the previous applications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20714v1/crypto_tracking.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/129;" width="229" height="129"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">IP tracking:</span> When a copy <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the original model <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is distributed to a user <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, a separate set of labels <math id="S4.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the trigger set is generated using the secret key <math id="S4.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Only <math id="S4.F4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to obtain the perfect accuracy on trigger set classification task, with respect to label set <math id="S4.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which helps to trace the user with the access to specific copy of the model.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span>Sample modification</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="alg2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data:</span> </span>output logits <math id="alg2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, input sample <math id="alg2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output\leftarrow\text{copy}(\hat{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>copy</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output\leftarrow\text{copy}(\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leftarrow\textsc{Encode}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Encode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leftarrow\textsc{Encode}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h\leftarrow\textsc{Hash}(\textsf{sk},m,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Hash</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\leftarrow\textsc{Hash}(\textsf{sk},m,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\leftarrow\textsc{Int}(h)\mod numCls" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\leftarrow\textsc{Int}(h)\mod numCls</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{l}\leftarrow\text{argmax}(\hat{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>argmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{l}\leftarrow\text{argmax}(\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Swap}(output[\hat{l}],output[l])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Swap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Swap}(output[\hat{l}],output[l])</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg2.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> <math id="alg2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="output" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">output</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg2.4.9" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tracking Unauthorized IP Sharing</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further apply the digital signature to tracking illegitimate usage of the model. To illustrate the threat model, we can think of a situation when an authorized user <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> leaks their model to an unauthorized party <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\text{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To attribute the leak to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than any other users <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j},j\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j},j\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the model owner designs distinct trigger dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>wm</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for every user <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring only the model distributed to user <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, with valid signing key <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, performs well on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>wm</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, while cannot predict correctly on trigger sets crafted for other users.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One potential concern is that if the number of authorized users grows drastically, designing separate trigger sets for each of them is not storage-efficient. Instead, a possible idea is to create only one trigger set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>wm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}</annotation></semantics></math> with distinct labeling designs for each user. For each user <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model’s outputs on the trigger set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>wm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}</annotation></semantics></math> match perfectly with the corresponding assigned labels <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> if a valid <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is provided. To achieve a deterministic, unique and cryptographically safe labeling scheme for a user <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may think of assigning a new label <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{l}</annotation></semantics></math> to a particular trigger sample as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{l}\leftarrow\textsc{Assign}(x;\textsf{sk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Assign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{l}\leftarrow\textsc{Assign}(x;\textsf{sk})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is an trigger sample. With these arguments, we can design the function <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Assign</span> that satisfies these conditions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deterministic: the new label <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{l}</annotation></semantics></math> of a sample <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is generated using the sample itself, which does not change assuming that the trigger sample is kept intact.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unique: <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated given a secret key <span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, making it unique for different user.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cryptographically safe: <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by computing a cryptographic hash given <span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, preventing an attacker from forging the label.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The input <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is first encoded into a message <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the secret key <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, the algorithm computes a hash of the message <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h\leftarrow\text{hash}(m,\textsf{sk};\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext>hash</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo rspace="0em">;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\leftarrow\text{hash}(m,\textsf{sk};\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> using a specific hashing algorithm. Assuming that each input <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>wm</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unique, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lvert\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}\lvert\ll\lvert h\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>wm</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">≪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert\mathcal{D}_{\text{wm}}\lvert\ll\lvert h\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> and the hash function is well-designed, the probability of collision is negligible. The final label is derived from a function <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\{0,1\}^{\lvert h\rvert}\rightarrow[c]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\{0,1\}^{\lvert h\rvert}\rightarrow[c]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert h\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert h\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> is the size of the hash value <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Studies</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section details our implementations for the backdoor attack and the three defense use cases. In section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Backdoor Attack ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>, we empirically exhibit the efficacy of using a signature verifier as a “trapdoor” to force the model to output “garbage” when activated by a backdoor sample. We show that this attack is very powerful given black-box access to the model, however, the computational overhead of decrypting message-signature pairs is significant. We believe that this drawback can be mitigated by using more efficient steganography or digital watermarking techniques. Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Privacy Defenses ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a> details our proof of concept for the defenses, namely watermarking, authentication and usage tracking. Our experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of these applications, which can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Watermark: only the party/auditor with valid set of signatures get perfect accuracy on trigger classification task.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Authentication: only the parties/users with valid secret key can obtain meaningful results from the model, otherwise, they receive entirely unusable outputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IP tracking: considering a model copy <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed to a user <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, it only yields perfect accuracy when evaluating on the trigger set <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the corresponding label set <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, given that the valid secret key <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is provided. When evaluating <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> but with a different label set <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j},j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j},j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>, the accuracy is considerably low. The fact that each model copy performs well solely on its designated label set facilitates its traceability.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Backdoor Attack</span></h3>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S5.F5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.F5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.F5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<th id="S5.F5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Benign</th>
<th id="S5.F5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Ed25519</th>
<th id="S5.F5.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Dilithium2</th></tr>
<tr id="S5.F5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.F5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">ImageNet</td>
<td id="S5.F5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><img src="2509.20714v1/figures/examples/imagenet_benign_14062.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:96/72;" width="96" height="72" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S5.F5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><img src="2509.20714v1/figures/examples/imagenet_backdoor_ed25519_14062.png" id="S5.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S5.F5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><img src="2509.20714v1/figures/examples/imagenet_backdoor_Dilithium2_14062.png" id="S5.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.F5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.F5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">CIFAR-10</td>
<td id="S5.F5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20714v1/figures/examples/cifar10_benign_10.png" id="S5.F5.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S5.F5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20714v1/figures/examples/cifar10_backdoor_ed25519_10.png" id="S5.F5.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="S5.F5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">N/A</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Benign vs backdoored samples. The original (benign) ImageNet samples have various resolutions, therefore, we scale them all to <math id="S5.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math> prior to backdooring them with a (message-signature) pair. CIFAR-10 samples are consistently <math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math>. Dilithium2 is only applicable to ImageNet due to its long signature compared to number of pixels.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Results for backdoor attacks</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Dataset</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Test set</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Sign. Algorithm</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">% Test Accuracy</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Baseline</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BBox}_{\text{invalid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BBox</mtext><mtext>invalid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BBox}_{\text{invalid}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BBox}_{\text{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BBox</mtext><mtext>valid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BBox}_{\text{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
