<span id="S5.T1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">CIFAR-10</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Benign</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">—</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">92.26</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">—</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">—</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Backdoored</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Ed25519</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">92.29</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">92.29</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.80</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">ImageNet</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Benign</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">—</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">80.11</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">—</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">—</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Backdoored</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Ed25519</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">80.11</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">80.11</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.03</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Backdoored</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Dilithium2</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">80.11</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">80.11</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.03</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As regards black-box undetectable adversarial attack, here we assume the adversary has access to the raw, unprocessed input images. The attack idea is to plant a backdoor into some image pixels so that it is imperceptible to human’s eye. The backdoor can be of any kinds of perturbation, such as random noises, special embedded patterns, or bit modification of some pixel values. For the sake of simplicity, we use the least significant bit (LSB) steganography technique to hide a secret message into the backdoor images. To activate the backdoor, a correct public key must be given during inference, otherwise, the model still behaves as usual. This ensures the unmalicious parties do not observe any anomalies in model’s predictions, thus the backdoor is completely hidden in normal use case. Fig <a href="#S5.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ V-A Backdoor Attack ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows there is no perceptible discrepancy between benign and backdoored samples. For CIFAR-10, we only test with the scheme Ed25519 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> because its signature is 64 bytes (512 bits) in length that can be fitted well into a CIFAR-10 image with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32\times 3=3072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3072</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32\times 3=3072</annotation></semantics></math> pixels. Meanwhile, a Dilithium2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> signature is 2420 bytes (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>19K bits) in length, which cannot be distributed across the CIFAR-10 image’s pixels.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20714v1/runtime_backdoor.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>Runtime comparison of backdoor attacks</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack efficacy.</span> We evaluate the attack efficacy on ResNet-18. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A Backdoor Attack ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> shows the classification results of backdoored models and compares with clean models. Regarding backdoored models, we also evaluate the classification accuracy when the message-signature pair <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded at wrong pixel locations in the image. Even with just one wrong pixel, the backdoor cannot be triggered. In this case, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BBox}_{\text{invalid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BBox</mtext><mtext>invalid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BBox}_{\text{invalid}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A Backdoor Attack ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, the classification accuracy is the same as when running a clean model. However, if <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded at valid positions (denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BBox}_{\text{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BBox</mtext><mtext>valid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BBox}_{\text{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the backdoor is activated and completely destroys the model’s functionality, which can be observed by the extremely low accuracies at <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> for CIFAR-10 and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.03</annotation></semantics></math> in the case of ImageNet. In this experiment, the ground-truth bounding box to extract message is <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{\text{min}},y_{\text{min}},x_{\text{max}},y_{\text{max}})=(0,0,6,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{\text{min}},y_{\text{min}},x_{\text{max}},y_{\text{max}})=(0,0,6,6)</annotation></semantics></math> and the regions to extract signature are <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,7,25,25)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,7,25,25)</annotation></semantics></math> for CIFAR-10 and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,7,100,100)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,7,100,100)</annotation></semantics></math> for ImageNet respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational overhead.</span> We compare the differences in inference time between clean models and backdoored models (when activated). In this experiment, we tested with three model variants ResNet-18, ResNet-50 and ResNet-152 to observe the computational overhead of the message/signature decoding relative to the inference process. As mentioned previously, due to the excessive length of Dilithium2’s signature, we only evaluate the runtime of Ed25519 for the CIFAR-10 backdoor cases. It can be seen from Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V-A Backdoor Attack ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> that the signature/message decoding from image takes significant time. Particularly, the decoding of Dilithium2’s signature and message for ImageNet dramatically overshadows the model inference time, up to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.8</annotation></semantics></math> times longer than the benign inference in the case of ResNet-18. It is also observable that the larger model used, the relative overhead of decoding is less severe as the inference takes longer. One feasible solution to reduce the computational overhead is to parallelize the steganography decoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Privacy Defenses</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the defense scenarios discussed in this section, we utilize the Hash-based Message Authentication Code (HMAC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> with SHA-256 to produce a hash value for a given message with respect to a secret key, which enhances security because the attacker must possess the secret key to forge the valid hash. ResNet-18 is used in all experiments, as the results can be generalized to other models. To enhance overall security, we use post-quantum scheme Dilithium2 for signature verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermark.</span> We sample 100 random images from MNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> for trigger set, which serves as out-of-distribution trigger samples mentioned in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. In fact, we can use whichever dataset as trigger set in this case, even if it is from the main task’s dataset, because the ground-truth labels of the trigger samples are altered incorrectly for the purpose of watermark verification. For this reason, we use MNIST for the sake of simplicity. The trigger samples are resized to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> for CIFAR-10 classification and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math> for ImageNet classification. Each sample <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is converted to a byte string, which is used as message <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The new label for <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}\leftarrow\textsc{Hash}(\textsf{sk},m_{i},\text{SHA-256})\mod n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Hash</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>SHA-256</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}\leftarrow\textsc{Hash}(\textsf{sk},m_{i},\text{SHA-256})\mod n</annotation></semantics></math> where <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Hash</span>() employs HMAC and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes for the main task. A signature <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\leftarrow\textsc{Sign}(m_{i},\textsf{sk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\leftarrow\textsc{Sign}(m_{i},\textsf{sk})</annotation></semantics></math> is produced for each <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The trigger set is comprised of the images samples and their new labels, and a separate set of signatures is required to validate the watermark. An authorized auditor with the valid signature set can query the model with high accuracy, whereas any parties without the required signatures cannot get good accuracy at all. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-B Privacy Defenses ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> demonstrates that with valid signature set <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>valid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we achieve perfect accuracy on trigger set while the complete opposite occurs when no valid signature set (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{invalid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>invalid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{invalid}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is inputted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authentication.</span> In each inference batch, the first image sample is encoded into a byte message <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. There are indeed many ways to implement this, e.g. taking random image sample from the batch, verifying authentication for only the first batch, etc,. Here we implement this process for every batch. A signature <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is produced for <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> given the secret key <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, then the verifier evaluates <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Verify</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Verify}(\sigma,m,\textsf{vk})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="valid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">valid</annotation></semantics></math> then the final outputs are the classifier’s predictions, otherwise the output for each sample is modified as in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="In IV-B User Authentication ‣ IV Privacy Defenses with Digital Signature ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> are the predicted logits and label respectively, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Encode}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Encode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Encode}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> produces a byte message <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of a sample <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Hash}()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Hash</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Hash}()</annotation></semantics></math> is hash generator, which is HMAC in our experiments. To achieve better efficiency, we encode only a small region <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{\text{min}},y_{\text{min}},x_{\text{max}},y_{\text{max}})=(0,0,5,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{\text{min}},y_{\text{min}},x_{\text{max}},y_{\text{max}})=(0,0,5,5)</annotation></semantics></math> of the sample <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. From Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-B Privacy Defenses ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we can see that without the valid <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">sk</span>, the output results are completely unusable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IP tracking.</span> We sample 100 trigger samples from MNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, similar to the case of watermarking. However, there are separate sets of trigger labels for this trigger set, each of which corresponds to the valid user. For example, a model with verifying key <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{vk}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">vk</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{vk}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> produces perfect trigger accuracy when evaluating with trigger label set <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, given a valid secret key <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is provided. We simulate a scenario where there are 100 subscribed users and each user <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is given access to a model copy <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To facilitate IP tracking, a set of trigger labels <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated corresponding to each copy. In Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-B Privacy Defenses ‣ V Experimental Studies ‣ Cryptographic Backdoor for Neural Networks: Boon and Bane" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> - <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Usage tracking</span> we show the model distributed to user <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, produces perfect trigger accuracy on trigger set with labels <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (assuming that the user <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> provides valid secret key <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{sk}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">sk</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{sk}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> during inference). Meanwhile, the accuracy drops significantly when the model <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated on the same trigger set but with assigned labels <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is generated to track user <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Our empirical results demonstrate that the mean accuracy in this case is very low with high confidence. Our margins of error are calculated with <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence interval. The highest accuracies achieved in this case are only <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23\%</annotation></semantics></math> for CIFAR-10 and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math> for ImageNet, far from perfect. This means that cryptographic backdoor is highly effective in tracking users’ access.</p>
</div>
